WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:03.501
-You look great. It is good to see you again.

00:00:03.501 --> 00:00:05.901
Since we've talked, "Army of Thieves," your movie,

00:00:05.901 --> 00:00:08.200
is the number-one Netflix movie

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:11.067
in 90-plus countries, something crazy.

00:00:11.067 --> 00:00:12.634
Congratulations.

00:00:12.634 --> 00:00:13.767
[ Cheers and applause ] I mean...

00:00:13.767 --> 00:00:15.701
-It's amazing. -I can tell --

00:00:15.701 --> 00:00:17.667
-I can already tell you've changed a little bit.

00:00:17.667 --> 00:00:20.400
This has gone to your head. The success has changed you.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:23.367
-That's not true. But --

00:00:23.367 --> 00:00:26.067
-Is anything different? -Oh, yeah.

00:00:26.067 --> 00:00:28.667
Um -- [ Chuckles ]

00:00:28.667 --> 00:00:31.634
When we arrived -- When I arrived last weekend in New York

00:00:31.634 --> 00:00:36.534
and I stepped out of the plane and I went to immigration,

00:00:36.534 --> 00:00:38.267
I met an officer, an immigration officer,

00:00:38.267 --> 00:00:41.400
and he was the first guy I met in America.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:43.300
And he was like, "Oh, my God.

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:45.667
Ludwig Dieter. 'Army of Thieves.'

00:00:45.667 --> 00:00:47.534
That's amazing. Can we get a selfie?"

00:00:47.534 --> 00:00:48.968
-Yeah.

00:00:48.968 --> 00:00:50.601
-And I was like, "Of course, yes, sir.

00:00:50.601 --> 00:00:53.133
Yes, let's do it. Let's do a selfie."

00:00:53.133 --> 00:00:56.767
And then I asked, "Sir, but do I have to stand in this line,

00:00:56.767 --> 00:00:59.167
or can I get through quickly?"

00:00:59.167 --> 00:01:03.534
And he said, "No. No, boy. You go in line."

00:01:03.534 --> 00:01:05.000
-Oh, wow. Wow.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.868
So, for a second, you got a glimpse of ultimate -- Yeah.

00:01:08.868 --> 00:01:10.467
I'm so happy you're here.

00:01:10.467 --> 00:01:12.701
You know I'm a big fan, and you're super talented.

00:01:12.701 --> 00:01:14.334
I follow you on all the socials,

00:01:14.334 --> 00:01:17.234
and you post very funny, interesting things.

00:01:17.234 --> 00:01:21.434
This is a photo you posted on Father's Day.

00:01:21.434 --> 00:01:22.901
From your children. -Yeah.

00:01:22.901 --> 00:01:25.834
-Look at this nice picture. They made you these homemade --

00:01:25.834 --> 00:01:27.834
Can you tell everyone what this means?

00:01:27.834 --> 00:01:30.400
-Okay, so, first I have to say

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:32.634
I love my kids, but now with that story --

00:01:32.634 --> 00:01:34.167
So, this is me.

00:01:34.167 --> 00:01:36.868
On Father's Day, my kids, they made me these beautiful medals.

00:01:36.868 --> 00:01:38.968
-Aww. A medal for their dad.

00:01:38.968 --> 00:01:41.200
-And look. That's so sweet.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:44.334
The right one, it says "Happy Father's Day."

00:01:44.334 --> 00:01:45.734
-Yeah.

00:01:45.734 --> 00:01:47.701
-And the left one is, "You're ugly."

00:01:47.701 --> 00:01:50.467
[ Laughter ]

00:01:50.467 --> 00:01:53.701
I love my kids. It's so nice to have kids, huh?

00:01:53.701 --> 00:01:55.400
It's so --

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:57.767
-At least they have senses of humor.

00:01:57.767 --> 00:02:01.734
How old are they now? Your daughter's a teenager?

00:02:01.734 --> 00:02:03.133
-Yeah, she's turning 14. And she's --

00:02:03.133 --> 00:02:05.400
I mean, that's a pretty tough time.

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:07.033
She's, like, pre-puberty.

00:02:07.033 --> 00:02:09.267
And even my boy, he's 8. Every day he --

00:02:09.267 --> 00:02:12.567
When she starts to argue about anything,

00:02:12.567 --> 00:02:15.033
he's just like... [Whispering] "Puberty, Dad."

00:02:15.033 --> 00:02:16.901
[ Laughter ] -Your son says that?

00:02:16.901 --> 00:02:18.567
-Yeah, my son. My son is, uh --

00:02:18.567 --> 00:02:22.267
-Are they embarrassed by Dad at 8 years old?

00:02:22.267 --> 00:02:24.834
No. The teenager is, right? -Yeah, the teenager girl.

00:02:24.834 --> 00:02:26.501
She's embarrassed about anything.

00:02:26.501 --> 00:02:28.167
-Yeah. -She's embarrassed about me.

00:02:28.167 --> 00:02:31.400
"Dad, you're like --

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:34.667
You're a horrible person." For no reason.

00:02:34.667 --> 00:02:36.234
-Yeah, a horrible person. For no reason.

00:02:36.234 --> 00:02:38.200
-It's just like -- You're standing in the kitchen.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:40.701
"Thank you. Good morning. Yeah."

00:02:40.701 --> 00:02:44.667
-But they must see what you do and go like, "Dad's cool."

00:02:44.667 --> 00:02:48.133
-You know -- No, the truth is -- I had a film in Germany --

00:02:48.133 --> 00:02:52.167
For example, there was a poster, and I was naked on the poster.

00:02:52.167 --> 00:02:55.000
And -- But the class of my daughter,

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:57.033
they had to go to a museum,

00:02:57.033 --> 00:02:59.701
and so they walked past this poster.

00:02:59.701 --> 00:03:02.734
And, you know, of me naked.

00:03:02.734 --> 00:03:05.200
-No. -On the poster.

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:08.000
And the teacher -- a female teacher --

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:12.300
she was just like, "That's your dad."

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:14.267
"Yes. That's my dad."

00:03:14.267 --> 00:03:16.467
-Yeah. "That's my dad right there." Yeah.

00:03:16.467 --> 00:03:19.033
I heard that you brought a game for me tonight.

00:03:19.033 --> 00:03:21.200
You know I love games. -That's true.

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:24.234
-What do we have in store? What do we have planned?

00:03:24.234 --> 00:03:26.000
-Okay, so, I know you had -- Like, there have been

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:29.334
a few German-speaking actors, guests on the show.

00:03:29.334 --> 00:03:30.467
-Yeah, of course.

00:03:30.467 --> 00:03:31.767
-And they quizzed you on German words.

00:03:31.767 --> 00:03:34.033
I would love to do the same. But because the movie --

00:03:34.033 --> 00:03:35.634
I'm here for the movie "Swimmers,"

00:03:35.634 --> 00:03:38.334
so I would love to do a quiz with you

00:03:38.334 --> 00:03:40.334
about -- with German related to swimming.

00:03:40.334 --> 00:03:42.000
German words related to swimming,

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:43.734
and you have to guess the meaning.

00:03:43.734 --> 00:03:45.567
-"Swimmers" is the name of your film,

00:03:45.567 --> 00:03:47.234
and so this is German words

00:03:47.234 --> 00:03:48.634
that have something to do with swimming.

00:03:48.634 --> 00:03:50.300
-Right. -I'm good at this.

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:53.100
[ Laughter ]

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:56.167
-Okay. So, German word quiz.

00:03:56.167 --> 00:03:57.801
Here's the first word. It's --

00:03:57.801 --> 00:03:59.968
Do you want me to say it, like -- Can I say it fast?

00:03:59.968 --> 00:04:03.033
-Yes. Can you say it fast?

00:04:03.033 --> 00:04:04.968
-I mean the word. -Yeah.

00:04:04.968 --> 00:04:07.267
-So, the first word is Beckenrandschwimmer.

00:04:07.267 --> 00:04:09.267
[ Laughter ] It's hard.

00:04:09.267 --> 00:04:11.033
Do you want me -- You know --

00:04:11.033 --> 00:04:14.067
-Beckenrandschwimmer. He played Ross on "Friends."

00:04:14.067 --> 00:04:15.901
[ Laughter ]

00:04:15.901 --> 00:04:17.033
I know. I love that guy.

00:04:17.033 --> 00:04:18.667
-Everything in German is dubbed,

00:04:18.667 --> 00:04:20.234
so I do it with my dubbing voice.

00:04:20.234 --> 00:04:23.133
Like -- Beckenrandschwimmer.

00:04:23.133 --> 00:04:24.601
-Wow.

00:04:24.601 --> 00:04:25.934
-So, does it mean, "A,"

00:04:25.934 --> 00:04:28.334
someone who gets great joy from swimming

00:04:28.334 --> 00:04:30.100
or, "B," someone who swims

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:32.400
by holding on to the edge of the pool?

00:04:34.234 --> 00:04:36.434
-Say it again. -Beckenrandschwimmer.

00:04:36.434 --> 00:04:38.167
♪ It's Beckenrandschwimmer ♪

00:04:38.167 --> 00:04:39.501
-Ah.

00:04:39.501 --> 00:04:41.934
♪ Beckenrandschwimmer, Beckenrandschwimmer ♪

00:04:41.934 --> 00:04:43.767
-♪ Beckenrandschwimmer ♪

00:04:43.767 --> 00:04:47.100
-They're not playing -- There's not music for this.

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:49.200
Beckenrandschwimmer. It sounds --

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:50.767
The way you said it sounded, like, nice.

00:04:50.767 --> 00:04:52.467
So I would say --

00:04:52.467 --> 00:04:53.901
-Someone who gets great joy from swimming?

00:04:53.901 --> 00:04:55.267
-Yes, great joy from swimming, I say.

00:04:55.267 --> 00:04:56.501
[ Buzzer ] -That's wrong.

00:04:56.501 --> 00:04:58.033
[ Sad tuba plays ]

00:04:58.033 --> 00:05:01.167
-There's a word for someone who swims and holds on to the edge?

00:05:01.167 --> 00:05:03.200
What?! That's a thing?

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:05.667
-Yeah. -That's a word --

00:05:05.667 --> 00:05:08.434
We don't have a word for that in America. Wow!

00:05:08.434 --> 00:05:11.734
Beckenrandschwimmer. Okay. Alright.

00:05:11.734 --> 00:05:15.234
-Next word is Schmetterlingsbeinschlag.

00:05:15.234 --> 00:05:18.133
-It sounds dirty. -It's dirty.

00:05:18.133 --> 00:05:20.634
It's, "A," butterfly kick,

00:05:20.634 --> 00:05:23.634
or, "B," the spring on a diving board.

00:05:23.634 --> 00:05:25.634
It's Schmetterlingsbeinschlag.

00:05:25.634 --> 00:05:27.300
-Schbetter--

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:29.534
-♪ Schmetterlingsbeinschlag ♪ -♪ Schmettermelings-- ♪

00:05:29.534 --> 00:05:31.434
-♪ Schmetterlingsbeinschlag ♪

00:05:31.434 --> 00:05:33.267
[ Laughter ]

00:05:33.267 --> 00:05:36.133
-Schmetterlingspraaang. Sproing!

00:05:36.133 --> 00:05:38.234
-Schmetterlingsbeinschlag.

00:05:38.234 --> 00:05:41.200
-Sproing! Sproing! Right?

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:43.133
It's not [speaking German gibberish]

00:05:43.133 --> 00:05:44.767
-No. It's more like -- It's more like beinschlag.

00:05:44.767 --> 00:05:48.400
-No, I think it's more like -- [speaking German gibberish]

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:50.934
I'm saying sproing, diving board.

00:05:50.934 --> 00:05:52.534
[ Buzzer ] Okay. Alright.

00:05:52.534 --> 00:05:54.734
[ Sad tuba plays ]

00:05:54.734 --> 00:05:57.067
-Ah. Okay. So.

00:05:57.067 --> 00:06:00.300
The last one, it's a little different.

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:02.100
Like, the English word for pool.

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:05.534
The German word for pool, yeah, is called "Becken."

00:06:05.534 --> 00:06:07.701
And "Becken" has multiple meanings.

00:06:07.701 --> 00:06:10.767
So, "Becken," does it mean, "A," pelvis and cymbals,

00:06:10.767 --> 00:06:12.868
or uvula and bowling pin?

00:06:12.868 --> 00:06:14.834
[ Laughter ]

00:06:14.834 --> 00:06:18.434
-I'm gonna say uvula and bowling pin.

00:06:18.434 --> 00:06:20.901
[ Buzzer ] What?! Are you kidding me?!

00:06:20.901 --> 00:06:23.701
The -- What? How did you make that up?

00:06:23.701 --> 00:06:25.434
Why would you make that up?

00:06:25.434 --> 00:06:27.534
I didn't know what I was supposed to say.

00:06:27.534 --> 00:06:30.701
I have no idea. We're not playing this game anymore.

00:06:30.701 --> 00:06:32.200
-We will never play this game again.

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:34.100
-No, we'll play a game again, but not this one.

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:35.334
Please. Thank you. -That's for you. Thank you.

00:06:35.334 --> 00:06:37.067
-Let's talk about your movie, "The Swimmers."

00:06:37.067 --> 00:06:38.934
-Yes. -You play a coach.

00:06:38.934 --> 00:06:40.801
[ Laughter ]

00:06:40.801 --> 00:06:42.834
I love that you played this with me.

00:06:42.834 --> 00:06:44.701
Do you swim in real life? Do you know these words?

00:06:44.701 --> 00:06:49.567
-I can float and I can dive. I'm good at floating.

00:06:49.567 --> 00:06:52.167
-But there's much more action in this movie. Yeah.

00:06:52.167 --> 00:06:54.701
-I can swim. I can swim a bit. I'm not a really good swimmer,

00:06:54.701 --> 00:06:58.000
but I look good standing in the ocean, in the water.

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:02.400
-Floating around. Yeah. Looking around.

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:06.834
How did you get the story for this script?

00:07:06.834 --> 00:07:09.434
-This is a -- It's a true story.

00:07:09.434 --> 00:07:13.267
In 2016, I met -- It's a film about two sisters,

00:07:13.267 --> 00:07:16.901
and they flew the Syrian war, yeah?

00:07:16.901 --> 00:07:20.801
And I met these girls in 2016 on an awards show.

00:07:20.801 --> 00:07:22.667
I received an award as a director,

00:07:22.667 --> 00:07:24.801
and I was super proud because it was my first award.

00:07:24.801 --> 00:07:25.901
But then they came on stage

00:07:25.901 --> 00:07:28.300
and received an award for being heroes

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:30.834
because they've been through this crazy journey.

00:07:30.834 --> 00:07:33.133
And I was like...

00:07:33.133 --> 00:07:35.200
[ Laughter ]

00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:36.534
-"Here's my director award."

00:07:36.534 --> 00:07:38.200
-"It's your award now." -Yeah, exactly.

00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:39.901
-And I met the girls for the first time,

00:07:39.901 --> 00:07:41.701
and I thought the story was amazing.

00:07:41.701 --> 00:07:45.300
And five years later, Working Title,

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:48.501
Eric Fellner, called me and said, "Matthias, the producer,

00:07:48.501 --> 00:07:50.667
we are doing this film called 'The Swimmers,'

00:07:50.667 --> 00:07:53.634
based on the true story of these two swimming sisters.

00:07:53.634 --> 00:07:55.334
And do you want to play the coach?"

00:07:55.334 --> 00:07:58.467
And I was like, "Oh, my -- "Yes! Where do I need to sign?

00:07:58.467 --> 00:07:59.901
Yes, I would love to do that film."

00:07:59.901 --> 00:08:05.367
-The idea of the film is that the engine goes out on the boat.

00:08:05.367 --> 00:08:06.868
-Yeah, yeah.

00:08:06.868 --> 00:08:09.100
-So they actually swim and pull the boat.

00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:11.234
-They pull the boat, and they save loads of people

00:08:11.234 --> 00:08:13.200
by swimming through the Mediterranean Sea

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:15.634
at night just to save all these people's lives.

00:08:15.634 --> 00:08:17.634
And they've been like 17 and 19 at the time.

00:08:17.634 --> 00:08:19.067
-It's unbelievable.

00:08:19.067 --> 00:08:21.834
It's an unbelievable, unbelievable film.

00:08:21.834 --> 00:08:24.767
I'm so glad you made this. I want to show everyone a clip.

00:08:24.767 --> 00:08:27.067
Here's Matthias Schweighofer in "The Swimmers."

00:08:27.067 --> 00:08:28.567
Take a look at this.

00:08:28.567 --> 00:08:30.868
-I swim 200-meter freestyle in 2:12

00:08:30.868 --> 00:08:33.767
and the 100-meter butterfly in 1:09.

00:08:33.767 --> 00:08:35.934
-Butterfly in 1:09. -Yes.

00:08:35.934 --> 00:08:37.400
-Is she kidding? -No, no.

00:08:37.400 --> 00:08:40.567
She swims. She swims.

00:08:40.567 --> 00:08:43.667
-Okay. Okay.

00:08:43.667 --> 00:08:46.200
I have a small break before my 11:00 session,

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:48.801
so in the pool if you're quick.

00:08:48.801 --> 00:08:50.801
-Thank you, thank you, thank you, thank you.

00:08:50.801 --> 00:08:51.868
-Okay.

00:08:51.868 --> 00:08:53.701
-But I don't have swimsuit.

00:08:55.901 --> 00:08:58.434
-You don't have a swimsuit?

00:08:58.434 --> 00:09:00.467
-And goggles.

00:09:00.467 --> 00:09:01.801
[ Conversing in foreign language ]

00:09:01.801 --> 00:09:04.200
-Thank you so much. Thank you, thank you, thank you.

00:09:04.200 --> 00:09:06.501
-Let's go.

00:09:06.501 --> 00:09:09.334
-Hey, hey. Um, what's your name?

00:09:09.334 --> 00:09:11.534
-Yusra Mardini.

00:09:11.534 --> 00:09:13.367
-And I'm Sarah.

00:09:13.367 --> 00:09:16.634
Who are you? -Sven.

00:09:16.634 --> 00:09:18.033
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:18.033 --> 00:09:19.834
-Matthias Schweighofer.

00:09:19.834 --> 00:09:23.200
"The Swimmers" is in select theaters Friday

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:25.133
and on Netflix November 23rd.

00:09:25.133 --> 00:09:27.300
We have stand-up from comedian Kurt Braunohler

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:29.133
when we come back. Stick around, everybody.

